From 888cd4d1bbd43ef8ae211d69759441bf27e448fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeff Law <jeffreyalaw@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 07:09:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [PR rtl-optimization/128910] Fix incorrect equivalence
 between SUBREGS with different promotion state

Wow, this one was fun.  I fully expected this to be a RISC-V target problem,
but in the end it's generic and very old.  It's just been latent for decades!

The core problem is exp_equiv_p (used by CSE and GCSE) returns true for SUBREG
expressions with different promotion states.

A RISC-V backend change in this cycle stopped pretending we always had zero
extension insns during expansion.  Instead the port now emits the shift pair
when the appropriate extensions are not available.

That caused the testcase from the PR to use expanded shift pairs for zero
extension.  We had an underlying SUBREG object in those newly exposed shifts.
In one context that SUBREG object had promoted state and in another context it
did not.  GCSE (incorrectly) determined the SUBREGs were equivalent and created
the dominating definition allowing the two instances to be removed.  The
representative instance GCSE inserted had the promotion state markers, but was
inserted into a point where the promoted state did not hold.  combine then came
along saw we had a shift pair implementing zero extension on a value that was
marked as already extended, so combine removed the zero extension.  Boom,
incorrect code.

Bootstrapped and regression tested on x86 and aarch64.  Also tested on
riscv32-elf and riscv64-elf.  Pushing to the trunk.

Assisted-By: Claude for dumpfile analysis

	PR rtl-optimization/128910
gcc/
	* cse.cc (exp_equiv_p): Return false for SUBREGs that have different
	promotion states.

gcc/testsuite
	* gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c: New test.

(cherry picked from commit 7565093dd76ecbbb9b5e664db38f01680b72ac1a)
---
 gcc/cse.cc                              |  9 +++++++++
 gcc/testsuite/gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 33 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 gcc/testsuite/gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c

diff --git a/gcc/cse.cc b/gcc/cse.cc
index 25b2bf500a96..a1987e43d8f1 100644
--- a/gcc/cse.cc
+++ b/gcc/cse.cc
@@ -2688,6 +2688,15 @@ exp_equiv_p (const_rtx x, const_rtx y, int validate, bool for_gcse)
 	}
       break;
 
+    case SUBREG:
+      /* Two SUBREGs of the same REG are not interchangeable if they differ
+	 in their promotion state.  */
+      if (SUBREG_PROMOTED_VAR_P (x) != SUBREG_PROMOTED_VAR_P (y)
+	  || (SUBREG_PROMOTED_VAR_P (x)
+	      && SUBREG_PROMOTED_GET (x) != SUBREG_PROMOTED_GET (y)))
+	return false;
+      break;
+
     /*  For commutative operations, check both orders.  */
     case PLUS:
     case MULT:
diff --git a/gcc/testsuite/gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..c42640081993
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/testsuite/gcc.dg/torture/pr128910.c
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+/* { dg-do run } */
+/* { dg-require-effective-target int32plus } */
+
+__attribute__((noipa)) int
+f (unsigned long long x, int b)
+{
+  int t = 0, r = 0;
+  unsigned lo = x;
+  for (int i = 0; i < 3 && (unsigned) x; i++)
+    {
+      if (b || t)
+	r = x == lo;
+      t = x = i != 0;
+    }
+  return r;
+}
+
+int
+main (void)
+{
+  if (f (0x100000001ULL, 1) != 0)
+    __builtin_abort ();
+  __builtin_exit (0);
+}
